  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Significant precipitation developing today and \npersisting into Tuesday night, with light wintry precipitation \npossible over Moosehead and North Woods areas today and this \nevening. The wintry precipitation is not expected to impact \ntravel, and flooding is not expected.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nNot much change in our thinking for the upcoming event. Early\nthis (Tuesday) morning, a much cooler airmass has worked in\nbehind a cold front. Today, clouds will increase and thicken\nfrom the west, with precipitation developing from west to east\nfrom late morning to early evening. The majority of the day will\nbe dry but cool along the New Brunswick border, but\nprecipitation will begin as early as about 10-11am from Bar\nHarbor to Bangor to Moosehead Lake. Cold air damming will be\nstrong over the area, and a cool rain is expected for most\nareas with cold air damming strong holding into the evening as\nsurface low pressure tracks from west to east along the coast. \nHowever, it should be cold enough from Moosehead Lake north to \nthe North Woods and maybe to the St John Valley for some snow, \nor rain mixed with snow, from onset today into the evening. \nTemperatures will be very marginal and think that where snow \ndoes fall, it will have trouble accumulating. Some higher \nelevations of the North Woods and into the Central Highlands \naround Katahdin could see a few inches, but most other places \nthat do see flakes will have little/no accumulation. Anticipate \nareas from Caribou south to Houlton and south along I-95 and \nover in Downeast will be all rain, albeit a cold rain. Warming \naloft is strong enough by about midnight that everywhere will go\nover to plain rain. Rain continues overnight Tuesday night \nespecially in the north, and some showers persist mainly \nWednesday morning in the north, while Downeast dries out. Storm \ntotal precipitation will be fairly impressive, with 2/3 inch to \n1.25 inch, with the higher totals being either along the coast \nor in the Central Highlands and Moosehead Lake region. This will\nnot be enough precipitation for flooding, though. Winds are not\na concern with this system.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Widespread rain is likely again Friday/Friday \nnight. Chance of accumulating wintry precipitation for northern \nareas and strong winds Friday night into Saturday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nA strong low pressure system is expected to approach our area\nfrom the west early Friday, bringing widespread precipitation to\nthe region. Current ensemble guidance shows a 50 to 60% chance \nof at least 0.5 inches of precipitation. Snow is also possible \nover the North with some light accumulations possible but the \namount of snow will depend heavily on the track the system \ntakes. If the storm tracks to the north of the area then \nsoutherly flow will dominate, bringing warmer air northward \nwhich will result in more rain. Alternatively, if the storm \ntracks further south over the Gulf of Maine then a more easterly\nwind will limit the amount of warm air transport, allowing for \nmore snowfall. Model guidance is currently split over which \nsolution will prevail. However, solutions with the storm \ntracking north favor a stronger system which as it departs will \nresult in some quite gusty winds Friday night into Saturday, \npossibly exceeding 30 mph. Regardless of exact track, winds are\nstill likely to be elevated in the wake of the system which in \ncombination with cold air coming in behind it will result in \nwind chills dipping into the 20s and teens.",
